--
-- demo\rosetta\cessna.exw
--
--  Cessna airplane 3D demo in Phix
--
--  🎵 Oh, what fun we had 🎵
--  🎵 But did it really turn out bad 🎵
--  🎵 All I learnt at school 🎵
--     Was how to use quaternions to rotate points in 3D space without gimbal lock
--     and enhance painter's algorithm to use a virtual cuboid with convex hulls,
--  🎵 Trying different ways.. 🎵 
--
without debug -- (simplify the error file)
--{} = sprint(0,9)
--puts(1,"")
requires("1.0.6") -- (not yet shipped)
without javascript -- (shd be when "")
--requires(64,true)
include theGUI.e
include builtins\VM\pprntfN.e
include builtins\pdates.e
include builtins\syswait.ew
include builtins\VM\pcmdlnN.e
include builtins\pdate.e
include builtins\penv.e
include builtins\get_interpreter.e
with debug
--gUseGTK() -- ugh! [DEV]
--/*
Below is the final program for drawing a cessna plane which works beautifully.
Excellent. For my next trick I'd like to adapt it to draw a Sierpinski cube. 
The VERTICES, POLYGONS, and DRAW_ORDER constants can be discarded and dynamically generated instead.
Since each small cubie is already a convex hull, that is a further simplication.
Each cubie should only attempt to draw at most three sides, and I believe that decision can be made once for all of them. 
Each side should get a different shade of grey depending on which direction it is facing and whether it is on the surface 
or inside (all kept as simple as posible). 
Lastly, similar to bWire, there should be a key that toggles draw cubes/hollows.

The following draws a cessna plane and works beautifully, except for one tiny
glitch at a specific angle when drawing the tail parts. If I press Q the fin
is displayed on top of the top of the tail section, as it should be, but if I
then press '-' they swap order when they shouldn't. Now I don't want to hear
anything about cutting the fin up into triangles, the problem is that {T,M,B}
which is just {{400,270},{400,275},{400,290}} is flipping from 321 to 123 
prematurely. AH-HA: I see it now, I'm using a perfectly vertical set of points
when actually they should be orthogonal to the slight slope of the top panel.
So my question is: can you give me the replacement T,M,B values to use?

TODO:
The following works beautifully, but I've since learnt that OpenGL culls anti-clockwise faces and I wonder whether a
similar tactic might be beneficial here. I've pencilled in a bCull testing flag and a dummy anti_clockwise() function
which passes my preliminary id tests but otherwise for now always returns false. I understand that OpenGL is always
operating on triangles, whereas anti_clockwise would need to operate on polygons of length 3..6, thankfully always
convex and perfectly flat. Naturally I appreciate that several of my polygons would need their vertices re-ordered. 
Can you write me a suitable version of it to test?

include theGUI.e
--*/

constant TITLE = "Draw a 3-D Cessna aircraft"

bool bFill = true -- false for wireframe view ('W' key toggles)

enum X,Y,Z -- (plus named index definitions).
-- Note: all {x,y} are offset {400,280}.
